1.8.17 Debugging Master Challenges

Challenge 1 — String

String a = new String("Java");
String b = new String("Java");

if (a == b) {
    // ...
}

Explain the bug and fix it.


---

Challenge 2 — Wrapper

Integer a = 127;
Integer b = 127;

Integer x = 1000;
Integer y = 1000;

Predict both == comparisons and explain why relying on this behavior is a trap.


---

Challenge 3 — Pass-by-value

void change(User user) {
    user.setName("Bob");
    user = new User("Charlie");
}

Predict what the caller observes.


---

Challenge 4 — Aliasing

List<String> a = new ArrayList<>();
List<String> b = a;

b.add("Java");

What does a contain and why?


---

Challenge 5 — Memory Retention

A long-lived executor stores a callback implemented as an inner class.

The outer service contains a large object graph.

Question:

> Why might the entire graph remain reachable?



The source specifically identifies threads/executors and callbacks as lifecycle-sensitive cases for inner classes. 


---

Challenge 6 — HashMap

Map<User, String> map = new HashMap<>();

User user = new User("Alex");
map.put(user, "Admin");

user.setName("Bob");

map.get(user);

Explain why this can fail if name participates in hashCode().


---

1.8.18 Production Decision Matrix

For every feature, be able to answer:

Feature	Main Production Question

Static nested class	Does it really need outer state?
Inner class	Could it accidentally retain outer state?
Local class	Is the helper truly local?
Anonymous class	Would lambda/named class be clearer?
Enum	Is business meaning independent of ordinal?
Annotation	Does runtime metadata justify the complexity?
Varargs	Is convenience worth allocation/API trade-offs?
Pass-by-value	Could mutation confuse callers?
References	Who owns this mutable object?
==	Am I testing identity intentionally?
equals()	Am I testing logical equality correctly?
hashCode()	Will this object be used in hash collections?
Identity	Is this an entity or value object?
Memory retention	What references keep this object alive?



