Don't mark Module 1.8 complete until you can:

[ ] Explain all four nested-class categories.

[ ] Distinguish static nested vs inner classes.

[ ] Explain outer-instance relationships.

[ ] Explain Outer.this.

[ ] Explain local-class scope.

[ ] Explain effectively-final capture.

[ ] Explain anonymous classes.

[ ] Compare anonymous classes with lambdas.

[ ] Implement enums with fields and behavior.

[ ] Explain enum initialization and identity.

[ ] Explain why ordinal should not normally be persisted.

[ ] Create custom annotations.

[ ] Explain SOURCE/CLASS/RUNTIME retention.

[ ] Explain annotation targets.

[ ] Process runtime annotations through reflection.

[ ] Explain varargs syntax and representation.

[ ] Explain varargs allocation.

[ ] Explain generic varargs and heap pollution.

[ ] Explain Java pass-by-value precisely.

[ ] Explain object-reference value copying.

[ ] Demonstrate mutation vs reassignment.

[ ] Explain aliasing.

[ ] Explain Java reference semantics without pointer-based misconceptions.

[ ] Explain == for primitives.

[ ] Explain == for references.

[ ] Explain equals().

[ ] Implement correct equals()/hashCode().

[ ] Explain String pooling.

[ ] Explain wrapper caching.

[ ] Explain identity vs logical equality.

[ ] Debug equality bugs.

[ ] Debug aliasing/reference bugs.

[ ] Diagnose memory retention.

[ ] Discuss performance implications.

[ ] Make production design decisions.

[ ] Complete basic → production coding exercises.

[ ] Defend decisions in senior-level interviews.


This matches the source's own final mastery gate, which requires everything from nested types and annotations through pass-by-value, equality, identity, memory retention, performance, production trade-offs and senior-level questions. 

⭐ Most important 1.8 concepts to master deeply

If you want to prioritize the highest-value areas:

1. Static nested class vs inner class


2. Inner-class outer-reference and memory retention


3. Local/anonymous class capture


4. Lambda vs anonymous class


5. Enum identity vs ordinal


6. Annotation retention + reflection


7. Varargs + generic heap pollution


8. Java is pass-by-value


9. Reference copying vs object copying


10. Aliasing


11. == vs equals()


12. String pool


13. Wrapper caching


14. equals/hashCode contract


15. Mutable keys


16. Identity vs logical equality


17. GC roots and memory retention



This gives 1.8 the same learning progression as 1.6 and 1.7, while retaining every subtopic from your curriculum. 
